<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Two fixes for the Android 15 edge-to-edge issue, so the tablet can pick one.
  The same two the PhotoCraft port shipped.

  Cause: the APK targets targetSdkVersion 35, and Android 15 enforces
  edge-to-edge for every app targeting 35+. The system stops insetting the
  window and draws the app underneath a transparent status bar. eframe/winit
  implements no inset handling (winit 0.30.13 has no Android safe-area API), so
  egui lays WordCraft's app bar and menu bar out at y=0 — behind the
  notification bar, where they are drawn but never receive touches.

  Both themes are one attribute deep; the difference is whether the status bar
  is made room for, or hidden entirely.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
      VARIANT A — keep the notification bar, put the app below it.

      `windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement` restores the old window fitting: the
      system insets the activity's window below the status bar again, so the
      NativeActivity surface begins underneath it and the menu bar lands where it
      can be tapped.

      Android 15 only. The platform marks this attribute deprecated in favour of
      apps consuming WindowInsets themselves, and Android 16 ignores it. The
      version-proof equivalent is a JNI call to
      WindowInsetsController.setOnApplyWindowInsetsListener plus a top spacer in
      egui, which is not implemented here.
    -->
    <style name="WordcraftStatusBar" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement">true</item>
        <!-- `windowSoftInputMode` has no home in cargo-apk's manifest schema, and none is needed:
             it is a *themeable* attribute (`attrs.xml` declares the flags and the platform's own
             base theme sets it as an item), so the theme can carry it. Verified to survive into the
             built resources as `0x0101022b=0x10`. See the note under WordcraftFullscreen for what it
             does and does not achieve on Android 15. -->
        <item name="android:windowSoftInputMode">adjustResize</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      VARIANT B — immersive, like a game: hide the status bar.

      `windowFullscreen` hides the status bar, so nothing overlays the menu bar
      and the canvas gets the whole screen height. This is the legacy mechanism,
      so it is less certain than variant A on Android 15 — if the status bar
      survives the edge-to-edge enforcement the menu bar stays unreachable. This
      is the variant that was confirmed working on the tablet for PhotoCraft.

      Note this hides only the *status* bar. Hiding the navigation bar too needs
      the immersive-mode API (WindowInsetsController.hide(Type.systemBars())),
      which is Java-only and would need a JNI bridge; there is no Java in this
      project.
    -->
    <style name="WordcraftFullscreen" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar.Fullscreen">
        <item name="android:windowFullscreen">true</item>
        <!-- See the note in WordcraftStatusBar: `adjustResize` asks the system to shrink the window
             for the keyboard and the taskbar instead of drawing them over the app. On Android 15 it
             is not enough on its own: the window manager honours the flag (`sim={adjust=resize}` in
             `dumpsys window`) but nothing consumes the insets — `mImeInsetsConsumed=false` — because
             targeting SDK 35 puts the app in charge of them, and consuming them needs a Java-side
             WindowInsets call this project has no bridge for. Kept because it is the right thing to
             ask for and other Android versions and devices do act on it. -->
        <item name="android:windowSoftInputMode">adjustResize</item>
    </style>

</resources>
